# chktex configuration for this conjecture. Run from this directory:
#
#     chktex -q statement.tex
#
# Three warnings are suppressed, checked hit by hit on 27 August 2026 rather
# than assumed, each with a 100% false-positive rate in this file:
#
#   12  "Interword spacing (`\ ') should perhaps be used." Fires at a genuine
#       sentence-ending period whose following token is a control sequence
#       (\emph{...}) rather than a capital letter, so chktex reads the period
#       as an abbreviation dot.  (1 hit)
#   13  "Intersentence spacing (`\@') should perhaps be used." Every remaining
#       hit is a `:' after an uppercase letter, inside a cited title, where
#       `\@' has no effect --- TeX's spacefactor logic applies only to `.',
#       `?' and `!'. The sentence-final `.' hits were fixed with `\@'
#       rather than suppressed, and lacheck is silent.  (1 hit)
#   38  "You should not use punctuation in front of quotes." Each hit is the final
#       character of a sentence quoted verbatim from the source paper. The
#       punctuation belongs to the quoted text; moving it out would misquote.  (2 hits)
#
# Everything else stays on. As of 27 August 2026 the file is clean under this
# config and `lacheck statement.tex` is silent.

CmdLine
{
    -n12
    -n13
    -n38
}
